1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,040
the real algebraic suffering begins,

2
00:00:03,040 --> 00:00:09,680
then q is going to be equal to that thing plus that thing.

3
00:00:09,680 --> 00:00:15,080
And if we go and do all of the algebraic manipulation

4
00:00:15,080 --> 00:00:24,040
to find out, so what, qi plus that equals a times 3n minus 1

5
00:00:24,040 --> 00:00:26,640
all over 4bn.

6
00:00:26,640 --> 00:00:30,960
Now, that's obvious to you that maybe you should be a mathematician.

7
00:00:30,960 --> 00:00:32,720
Because if you're just looking, of course,

8
00:00:32,720 --> 00:00:36,160
a times 3 minus 1, all right.

9
00:00:36,160 --> 00:00:38,440
And then from that, we go back to the demand function,

10
00:00:38,440 --> 00:00:40,360
and we find out p.

11
00:00:40,360 --> 00:00:42,160
And so p for the demand function is just

12
00:00:42,160 --> 00:00:46,880
going to be a times n plus 1 over 4n.

13
00:00:51,280 --> 00:00:53,760
And then we just go and multiply these things.

14
00:00:53,760 --> 00:00:57,360
We're going to multiply pq, p times the market price

15
00:00:57,360 --> 00:01:01,240
is little q, to find out what your defection problems would be.

16
00:01:01,240 --> 00:01:03,880
And it comes out to that thing in the notes, a squared times n

17
00:01:03,880 --> 00:01:06,960
plus 1 squared all over 16bn squared.

18
00:01:06,960 --> 00:01:10,680
And then for collusion, now we can plug everything in.

19
00:01:10,680 --> 00:01:14,360
So collusion will be sustainable if this thing here, right,

20
00:01:14,360 --> 00:01:17,720
if we go and plug that in for pi m,

21
00:01:17,720 --> 00:01:21,200
and we go and we plug that thing that I told you in for pi d,

22
00:01:21,200 --> 00:01:25,040
and we go and put this in for pi c.

23
00:01:25,040 --> 00:01:28,440
So we plug in every single profit function

24
00:01:28,440 --> 00:01:30,480
to find out the profits.

25
00:01:30,480 --> 00:01:35,520
And then we get that giant mess in part j,

26
00:01:35,520 --> 00:01:37,520
which I will not read, because it's a book.

27
00:01:37,520 --> 00:01:40,040
And if you look at that, you may get into a panic,

28
00:01:40,040 --> 00:01:43,480
because it appears to be a fourth degree equation, right.

29
00:01:43,480 --> 00:01:47,040
And who here knows how to solve a fourth degree equation?

30
00:01:47,040 --> 00:01:47,760
Yes.

31
00:01:47,760 --> 00:01:49,720
There is a book that explains it.

32
00:01:49,720 --> 00:01:51,400
The formula is about two pages long.

33
00:01:51,400 --> 00:01:53,000
I'm not reading it.

34
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:58,880
All right, so economists never solve fourth degree equations.

35
00:01:58,880 --> 00:02:01,240
I have seen economists go and change a model just

36
00:02:01,240 --> 00:02:03,560
to make sure they wouldn't need to have a fourth degree

37
00:02:03,560 --> 00:02:08,400
equation solved, because they'd go, well, why not?

38
00:02:08,400 --> 00:02:10,240
Of course, maybe the world does require you

39
00:02:10,240 --> 00:02:11,840
to solve fourth degree equations.

40
00:02:11,840 --> 00:02:13,500
I think this is a case where you really

41
00:02:13,500 --> 00:02:16,920
do have to give a sociological slash economic theory of people

42
00:02:16,920 --> 00:02:20,520
saying, look, people in my professional subculture

43
00:02:20,520 --> 00:02:22,120
don't solve fourth degree equations.

44
00:02:22,120 --> 00:02:23,280
It's not done.

45
00:02:23,280 --> 00:02:26,120
So if I go and do it, I'm not going to get published.

46
00:02:26,120 --> 00:02:29,560
And then I will not get desirable material possessions

47
00:02:29,560 --> 00:02:30,800
and status.

48
00:02:30,800 --> 00:02:32,800
So I'm not going to change the model

49
00:02:32,800 --> 00:02:34,760
to make sure that it doesn't work out

50
00:02:34,760 --> 00:02:37,080
into a fourth degree model.

51
00:02:37,080 --> 00:02:39,160
This is true for theory.

52
00:02:39,160 --> 00:02:40,700
In empirics, on the other hand, there

53
00:02:40,700 --> 00:02:42,600
are some cases where you're required

54
00:02:42,600 --> 00:02:45,240
to do a fourth degree equation.

55
00:02:45,240 --> 00:02:48,560
20 years ago, there was a paper saying that experience

56
00:02:48,560 --> 00:02:50,680
goes and follows a quartic.

57
00:02:50,680 --> 00:02:53,480
And ever since, technically common papers

58
00:02:53,480 --> 00:02:56,040
put in experience, experience squared, experience cubed,

59
00:02:56,040 --> 00:02:59,680
experience to the fourth power as control variables.

60
00:02:59,680 --> 00:03:01,280
And if you ever wondered, when someone

61
00:03:01,280 --> 00:03:04,400
says I'm going to put in a quartic in experience, why?

62
00:03:04,400 --> 00:03:07,000
Because of this paper 20 years ago said that you should do it,

63
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:08,600
and now everybody does it.

64
00:03:08,600 --> 00:03:13,920
Of course, it's much easier to go and do that in an OLS

65
00:03:13,920 --> 00:03:15,840
or something than it is to go and get

66
00:03:15,840 --> 00:03:18,600
the formula for getting an analytical solution to an

67
00:03:18,600 --> 00:03:20,720
equation.

68
00:03:20,720 --> 00:03:22,360
But in any case, if you look at this

69
00:03:22,360 --> 00:03:24,280
and think you've got a fourth degree equation,

70
00:03:24,280 --> 00:03:27,480
you are wrong, actually, because we're not solving for n.

71
00:03:27,480 --> 00:03:29,200
We're solving for beta.

72
00:03:29,200 --> 00:03:33,080
So phew, we don't actually need the equation for a fourth

73
00:03:33,080 --> 00:03:35,040
degree equation.

74
00:03:35,040 --> 00:03:37,920
We can just go and use our regular simple math

75
00:03:37,920 --> 00:03:41,040
that we already know, and then we can memorize.

76
00:03:41,040 --> 00:03:43,560
And we get this value of beta, which I will say

77
00:03:43,560 --> 00:03:44,640
is totally counterintuitive.

78
00:03:44,640 --> 00:03:45,880
But if you put in some numbers, then you'll

79
00:03:45,880 --> 00:03:47,520
see the numbers make sense.

80
00:03:47,520 --> 00:03:50,760
If I have n is 2, then beta is 1 by 3.

81
00:03:50,760 --> 00:03:54,160
That's the first value.

82
00:03:54,160 --> 00:03:58,360
So that is Carnot collusion for you.

83
00:03:58,360 --> 00:03:59,880
Now, after doing all this math, it's

84
00:03:59,880 --> 00:04:02,440
worth pointing out that all the math ignores a bunch

85
00:04:02,440 --> 00:04:05,920
of super important things.

86
00:04:05,920 --> 00:04:09,000
And why would the math ignore super important things?

87
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:11,880
Well, because first of all, the math

88
00:04:11,880 --> 00:04:14,400
is not empirical in the first place.

89
00:04:14,400 --> 00:04:17,840
So it isn't really designed to go and assess

90
00:04:17,840 --> 00:04:19,320
what's important in the real world.

91
00:04:19,320 --> 00:04:20,940
And then secondly, the important things

92
00:04:20,940 --> 00:04:24,720
might actually not require a lot of mathematical ability

93
00:04:24,720 --> 00:04:25,240
to discuss.

94
00:04:25,240 --> 00:04:28,720
And so if you think that possibly economists sometimes

95
00:04:28,720 --> 00:04:32,160
put in extra math just to signal how smart they are in papers,

96
00:04:32,160 --> 00:04:36,560
anyone here holds this cynical and despicable theory?

97
00:04:36,560 --> 00:04:41,480
That I have heard famous economists admit in public,

98
00:04:41,480 --> 00:04:43,520
or the Ashenfelter would sometimes say,

99
00:04:43,520 --> 00:04:45,860
and now this is the part where I added in some needless

100
00:04:45,860 --> 00:04:48,320
complexity to get published.

101
00:04:48,320 --> 00:04:52,240
So I suppose you could be lying about deliberately putting

102
00:04:52,240 --> 00:04:54,080
in some useless math into a paper.

103
00:04:54,080 --> 00:04:57,800
But you seem totally honest to me.

104
00:04:57,800 --> 00:05:00,520
So the main thing that all of this ignores

105
00:05:00,520 --> 00:05:03,640
is new entry, which almost any empirical discussion

106
00:05:03,640 --> 00:05:06,680
of collusion will say is incredibly important.

107
00:05:06,680 --> 00:05:09,120
Because it's one thing just to get

108
00:05:09,120 --> 00:05:11,800
unanimity, which is hard in the first place.

109
00:05:11,800 --> 00:05:13,880
So if there's five firms in the industry,

110
00:05:13,880 --> 00:05:17,840
you have to get all five of them to agree to the collusion, which

111
00:05:17,840 --> 00:05:19,280
is hard enough.

112
00:05:19,280 --> 00:05:22,400
So I always give my students the challenge of,

113
00:05:22,400 --> 00:05:24,320
you guys can all collude in the amount of work

114
00:05:24,320 --> 00:05:26,520
that you do, since this class is great and on a curve.

115
00:05:26,520 --> 00:05:28,840
You can all do less work, have more fun,

116
00:05:28,840 --> 00:05:30,440
and then get the same grade distribution

117
00:05:30,440 --> 00:05:32,160
as you would have otherwise.

118
00:05:32,160 --> 00:05:34,960
I've never seen even a hint that students are successfully

119
00:05:34,960 --> 00:05:36,280
colluding.

120
00:05:36,280 --> 00:05:39,160
Once I did teach a class with only four students in it,

121
00:05:39,160 --> 00:05:41,960
and then I was like, maybe I should not

122
00:05:41,960 --> 00:05:45,000
taunt them in this way, because maybe they would.

123
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:47,400
All I can say is when I looked at the exams,

124
00:05:47,400 --> 00:05:48,920
at least one student didn't collude.

125
00:05:48,920 --> 00:05:53,560
One student had a score that exceeded that

126
00:05:53,560 --> 00:05:54,360
of the other three.

127
00:05:54,360 --> 00:05:56,800
Maybe they colluded, and he drank them.

128
00:05:56,800 --> 00:05:58,640
But in any case, there's the general problem

129
00:05:58,640 --> 00:06:00,760
of getting unanimity among all firms,

130
00:06:00,760 --> 00:06:03,880
because if all firms but one fail to cooperate,

131
00:06:03,880 --> 00:06:06,000
then it's very hard to make it work.

132
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:08,200
But on top of it, even if you get all existing firms

133
00:06:08,200 --> 00:06:10,200
in the industry, there's a problem of new entry.

134
00:06:10,200 --> 00:06:12,880
Because if you do manage to get profits up and usually high,

135
00:06:12,880 --> 00:06:14,600
the normal thing for other outside firms

136
00:06:14,600 --> 00:06:16,600
to want to do is say, look, there's high profits.

137
00:06:16,600 --> 00:06:17,480
I want to try.

138
00:06:17,480 --> 00:06:19,400
Again, in principle, you could go and predate on them

139
00:06:19,400 --> 00:06:20,400
as soon as they show up.

140
00:06:20,400 --> 00:06:22,560
But then you need to have a really effective cartel,

141
00:06:22,560 --> 00:06:24,320
a cartel that not only includes everyone,

142
00:06:24,320 --> 00:06:28,040
but where someone snaps his fingers, and then new entry.

143
00:06:28,040 --> 00:06:30,040
Let's all unite against them now.

144
00:06:30,040 --> 00:06:31,160
And then, OK, they're gone.

145
00:06:31,160 --> 00:06:33,540
All right, now go back and resume our regularly scheduled

146
00:06:33,540 --> 00:06:34,040
collusion.

147
00:06:34,040 --> 00:06:38,920
You could try that, but in the real world, not too likely.

148
00:06:38,920 --> 00:06:42,320
If you go in, so very often, the most interesting,

149
00:06:42,320 --> 00:06:43,840
plain ways to learn about collusion

150
00:06:43,840 --> 00:06:46,040
is to go and read the history, economic history

151
00:06:46,040 --> 00:06:47,400
before antitrust.

152
00:06:47,400 --> 00:06:49,360
Because back in those days, there

153
00:06:49,360 --> 00:06:51,640
would be very little dispute about what the facts are.

154
00:06:51,640 --> 00:06:54,240
Today, if you want to collude, you need to do it secretly.

155
00:06:54,240 --> 00:06:55,740
And if people are doing it secretly,

156
00:06:55,740 --> 00:06:57,840
then there can always be some people saying,

157
00:06:57,840 --> 00:06:59,800
it happened in secret, and other people saying,

158
00:06:59,800 --> 00:07:01,880
no, it didn't happen at all.

159
00:07:01,880 --> 00:07:04,000
But in the 19th century, you can often go and see

160
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:05,800
people in the newspaper saying, we're

161
00:07:05,800 --> 00:07:09,760
trying to go and reduce leather production to raise prices.

162
00:07:09,760 --> 00:07:10,960
And you can see what happened.

163
00:07:10,960 --> 00:07:14,760
And all the problems that you hear about in a good econ

164
00:07:14,760 --> 00:07:19,600
class are very evident in 19th century cartels, where, yes,

165
00:07:19,600 --> 00:07:21,400
first of all, there's the trouble of getting

166
00:07:21,400 --> 00:07:22,840
all the firms in the industry in.

167
00:07:22,840 --> 00:07:24,920
And then there's the trouble of new entry.

168
00:07:24,920 --> 00:07:27,420
And one that's one particularly interesting kind of new entry

169
00:07:27,420 --> 00:07:30,640
is that very often, you would go and buy someone out,

170
00:07:30,640 --> 00:07:33,140
and then they would just start, take the money you gave them,

171
00:07:33,140 --> 00:07:35,440
and build a new firm, and then start competing with you.

172
00:07:35,440 --> 00:07:38,520
Usually, there would be an early version of a non-compete clause

173
00:07:38,520 --> 00:07:40,060
where they would promise to not enter

174
00:07:40,060 --> 00:07:41,560
the industry for five years.

175
00:07:41,560 --> 00:07:43,400
But again, in the real world, it would often

176
00:07:43,400 --> 00:07:47,800
take three or four years just to get the cartel working

177
00:07:47,800 --> 00:07:48,880
and functional.

178
00:07:48,880 --> 00:07:52,640
And then a guy says, well, one year from today,

179
00:07:52,640 --> 00:07:55,440
I'm opening the doors for my new firm.

180
00:07:55,440 --> 00:07:59,040
So they had a great deal of trouble.

181
00:07:59,040 --> 00:08:00,440
So questions about collusion?

182
00:08:00,440 --> 00:08:03,240
Anyone wondering about collusion?

183
00:08:03,240 --> 00:08:04,560
And moving on.

184
00:08:04,560 --> 00:08:06,280
So public goods and game theory.

185
00:08:06,280 --> 00:08:08,440
So I assume everyone here has heard about public goods

186
00:08:08,440 --> 00:08:09,800
and externalities at some point.

187
00:08:09,800 --> 00:08:12,440
All right, good.

188
00:08:12,440 --> 00:08:15,720
So there's a lot of treatments that emphasize non-rivalry,

189
00:08:15,720 --> 00:08:18,160
although actually, non-rivalry intrinsically

190
00:08:18,160 --> 00:08:20,160
has absolutely nothing at all to do with this.

191
00:08:20,160 --> 00:08:23,280
You can have a totally private good with non-rivalry,

192
00:08:23,280 --> 00:08:26,000
as long as you don't have perfect competition.

193
00:08:26,000 --> 00:08:28,680
So right here in this Cournot model we talked about,

194
00:08:28,680 --> 00:08:29,880
we got zero marginal cost.

195
00:08:29,880 --> 00:08:33,040
So is there non-rivalry?

196
00:08:33,040 --> 00:08:36,200
You could produce one more unit without actually going

197
00:08:36,200 --> 00:08:38,360
and taking away from anybody else.

198
00:08:38,360 --> 00:08:42,000
And there are many firms that we think of as private

199
00:08:42,000 --> 00:08:44,520
where actually they're clearly non-rival.

200
00:08:44,520 --> 00:08:48,280
So go to a movie theater during the matinee, a bunch of MTCs,

201
00:08:48,280 --> 00:08:49,960
so non-rival.

202
00:08:49,960 --> 00:08:51,540
In fact, the more you think about it,

203
00:08:51,540 --> 00:08:53,480
the harder it is to find rivalry.

204
00:08:53,480 --> 00:08:56,280
Because suppose you go to a restaurant

205
00:08:56,280 --> 00:08:57,280
and you order some food.

206
00:08:57,280 --> 00:08:58,580
You might see the food's rival.

207
00:08:58,580 --> 00:08:59,840
It's like, well, wait a second.

208
00:08:59,840 --> 00:09:02,400
What was going to happen to that food if you didn't order it?

209
00:09:02,400 --> 00:09:04,480
A lot of times it was just going to get thrown out

210
00:09:04,480 --> 00:09:05,440
at the end of the day.

211
00:09:05,440 --> 00:09:09,160
So maybe the food was rival too.

212
00:09:09,160 --> 00:09:09,660
Awesome.

213
00:09:09,660 --> 00:09:12,200
You look puzzled.

214
00:09:12,200 --> 00:09:17,960
Very often a restaurant will make 100 meals.

215
00:09:17,960 --> 00:09:20,560
And then if 90 people show up, what

216
00:09:20,560 --> 00:09:22,280
do they do with the other 10 meals?

217
00:09:22,280 --> 00:09:23,640
They throw them away.

218
00:09:23,640 --> 00:09:26,360
So then if person 91 comes up, the marginal cost

219
00:09:26,360 --> 00:09:30,800
of getting that food is basically zero.

220
00:09:30,800 --> 00:09:33,000
So anyway, a lot of treatments talk about non-rivalry.

221
00:09:33,000 --> 00:09:35,440
But really, non-excludability is what

222
00:09:35,440 --> 00:09:38,880
makes these cases special and interesting.

223
00:09:38,880 --> 00:09:40,700
And again, with non-excludability,

224
00:09:40,700 --> 00:09:43,320
there is this basic logic of selfishness,

225
00:09:43,320 --> 00:09:45,520
which is by definition of this non-rivalry,

226
00:09:45,520 --> 00:09:47,840
that there's no, or assuming non-excludability,

227
00:09:47,840 --> 00:09:50,920
there's no feasible way to exclude non-payers.

228
00:09:50,920 --> 00:09:54,480
And then if there's no way to exclude non-payers

229
00:09:54,480 --> 00:09:58,960
and people are selfish, what will the selfish people do?

230
00:09:58,960 --> 00:09:59,460
Yes.

231
00:09:59,460 --> 00:10:02,640
So wait, can I get the benefit whether or not I pay?

232
00:10:02,640 --> 00:10:03,320
Yes.

233
00:10:03,320 --> 00:10:04,440
And I'm selfish?

234
00:10:04,440 --> 00:10:05,440
Yes.

235
00:10:05,440 --> 00:10:06,880
Then I guess I won't pay.

236
00:10:06,880 --> 00:10:08,560
I'll just keep the money.

237
00:10:08,560 --> 00:10:10,880
And again, imagine that you actually pass a hat.

238
00:10:10,880 --> 00:10:12,360
And you invite people, put in money

239
00:10:12,360 --> 00:10:14,600
to contribute to however much you want.

240
00:10:14,600 --> 00:10:16,800
And the selfish people all put in zero.

241
00:10:16,800 --> 00:10:18,640
And then you go to a producer and say,

242
00:10:18,640 --> 00:10:20,640
would you produce this public good in exchange

243
00:10:20,640 --> 00:10:22,920
for the contents of this hat?

244
00:10:22,920 --> 00:10:24,840
And then he says, show me what's in the hat.

245
00:10:24,840 --> 00:10:26,280
Sorry, there's nothing in the hat.

246
00:10:26,280 --> 00:10:27,440
All right, so that's the end of it.

247
00:10:27,440 --> 00:10:28,600
There's no one to pay for it.

248
00:10:28,600 --> 00:10:30,800
Well, selfish producers are not going to provide it.

249
00:10:33,000 --> 00:10:35,840
So this does lead us to standard diagrams

250
00:10:35,840 --> 00:10:38,880
for externalities of public goods.

251
00:10:44,880 --> 00:10:47,440
So positive and negative externalities are really easy.

252
00:10:47,440 --> 00:10:49,100
There's actually two ways you can do it,

253
00:10:49,100 --> 00:10:53,120
but I'm going to show you the easiest way of all I do.

254
00:10:53,120 --> 00:10:58,120
So this is just quantity, price, supply to mend.

255
00:10:58,120 --> 00:11:01,160
And then the idea of a positive externality

256
00:11:01,160 --> 00:11:05,240
is that there's some additional benefits that go to bystanders,

257
00:11:05,240 --> 00:11:08,040
people who don't have to pay to get them.

258
00:11:08,040 --> 00:11:10,600
And so we can just add a social benefits curve

259
00:11:10,600 --> 00:11:13,280
on top, which includes the benefits to the paying

260
00:11:13,280 --> 00:11:17,880
customers plus all the benefits to the bystanders.

261
00:11:17,880 --> 00:11:20,280
All the benefits to the bystanders.

262
00:11:20,280 --> 00:11:24,240
And then, since people in markets are selfish,

263
00:11:24,240 --> 00:11:27,320
where do they produce given all these bystanders around

264
00:11:27,320 --> 00:11:29,360
who enjoy what they do for free?

265
00:11:29,360 --> 00:11:30,760
Intersection of supply and demand.

266
00:11:30,760 --> 00:11:32,080
Intersection of supply and demand, yes.

267
00:11:32,080 --> 00:11:33,760
So wait, so you're telling me there's a bunch of bystanders

268
00:11:33,760 --> 00:11:36,640
and they don't pay us anything, but there's a lot of them.

269
00:11:36,640 --> 00:11:37,680
How many?

270
00:11:37,680 --> 00:11:38,880
Trillion.

271
00:11:38,880 --> 00:11:40,520
I think I'll still produce at the intersection of supply

272
00:11:40,520 --> 00:11:41,760
and demand.

273
00:11:41,760 --> 00:11:43,560
So they don't do anything for me,

274
00:11:43,560 --> 00:11:45,800
so why should I do anything for them?

275
00:11:45,800 --> 00:11:49,960
But the efficient outcome, the Calgary's efficient outcome

276
00:11:49,960 --> 00:11:52,440
is going to be the intersection of supply and social benefits

277
00:11:52,440 --> 00:11:55,360
because for these marginal units here,

278
00:11:55,360 --> 00:11:57,760
the social benefits are actually greater.

279
00:11:57,760 --> 00:11:59,240
Why then are markets ignoring them?

280
00:11:59,240 --> 00:12:00,400
Because they're bystanders.

281
00:12:00,400 --> 00:12:02,200
Because they're people who are sitting,

282
00:12:02,200 --> 00:12:06,920
enjoying these monocultural benefits, not contributing.

283
00:12:06,920 --> 00:12:08,000
And again, why would they?

284
00:12:08,000 --> 00:12:10,560
Why would they contribute when whatever they're going to get,

285
00:12:10,560 --> 00:12:11,480
they get for free?

286
00:12:11,480 --> 00:12:13,320
And if they were to go and offer some money,

287
00:12:13,320 --> 00:12:16,800
they wouldn't get any more because it's just one person.

288
00:12:16,800 --> 00:12:18,640
So that case leads to positive externalities.

289
00:12:18,640 --> 00:12:21,080
Then the deadweight costs or the deadweight losses

290
00:12:21,080 --> 00:12:24,400
will be shown here, where again, basically we're

291
00:12:24,400 --> 00:12:29,800
losing all of this value where social benefits exceed

292
00:12:29,800 --> 00:12:31,400
the social costs.

293
00:12:31,400 --> 00:12:34,680
So supply shows social costs, social benefits

294
00:12:34,680 --> 00:12:36,760
show social benefits, so all of these units don't happen.

295
00:12:36,760 --> 00:12:38,880
All of these units on the other end do happen.

296
00:12:38,880 --> 00:12:40,160
So even though there's an externality,

297
00:12:40,160 --> 00:12:41,360
there's no efficiency loss there.

298
00:12:41,360 --> 00:12:44,120
Then it's just the bystanders get to enjoy themselves.

299
00:12:44,120 --> 00:12:47,360
And the burden is borne by the producers and the pet

300
00:12:47,360 --> 00:12:49,080
customers.

301
00:12:49,080 --> 00:12:51,400
So that's positive externalities.

302
00:12:51,400 --> 00:12:53,760
And then we've got the corresponding case

303
00:12:53,760 --> 00:12:55,520
of the negative externalities.

304
00:12:55,520 --> 00:12:58,760
So again, here we got supply, here we got demand.

305
00:12:58,760 --> 00:13:03,080
And now we've got social benefits below the demand curve.

306
00:13:03,080 --> 00:13:05,120
And these are cases where there's bystanders who

307
00:13:05,120 --> 00:13:07,280
are suffering, bystanders who are suffering,

308
00:13:07,280 --> 00:13:11,000
who find it unpleasant in some way.

309
00:13:11,000 --> 00:13:14,400
So and then again, what does the market do?

310
00:13:14,400 --> 00:13:16,680
Market goes to the intersection of which two curves?

311
00:13:16,680 --> 00:13:17,440
Supply and demand.

312
00:13:17,440 --> 00:13:19,040
Supply and demand, because that's

313
00:13:19,040 --> 00:13:20,560
what the pet customers care about.

314
00:13:20,560 --> 00:13:22,720
You say, look, but there's a bunch of freeloaders

315
00:13:22,720 --> 00:13:25,080
who are suffering.

316
00:13:25,080 --> 00:13:27,160
Wait, what are freeloaders again?

317
00:13:27,160 --> 00:13:28,800
The people who don't pay you any money,

318
00:13:28,800 --> 00:13:30,920
don't do anything for you?

319
00:13:30,920 --> 00:13:34,040
I'm in business to make money, so those people

320
00:13:34,040 --> 00:13:36,880
are irrelevant as far as I'm concerned.

321
00:13:36,880 --> 00:13:38,840
So in that case, what you produce here, again,

322
00:13:38,840 --> 00:13:41,360
the efficient thing is at the intersection of social benefits

323
00:13:41,360 --> 00:13:42,280
and supply.

324
00:13:42,280 --> 00:13:44,680
And again, the problem now is that we're

325
00:13:44,680 --> 00:13:49,400
losing a bunch of units where the social value is down here,

326
00:13:49,400 --> 00:13:51,320
but the social cost is up there.

327
00:13:51,320 --> 00:13:55,000
And so then we have those deadweight losses.

328
00:13:55,000 --> 00:13:57,000
So that's the negative externality.

329
00:14:00,520 --> 00:14:03,600
All right, now, where do public goods

330
00:14:03,600 --> 00:14:06,000
and the corresponding category of public bads come in?

331
00:14:06,000 --> 00:14:08,800
So here I'm going to tell you something

332
00:14:08,800 --> 00:14:10,920
weird that you may have never heard before.

333
00:14:10,920 --> 00:14:13,040
And that is there's two actual ways

334
00:14:13,040 --> 00:14:15,320
that economists use the concept of public goods and public

335
00:14:15,320 --> 00:14:15,840
bads.

336
00:14:15,840 --> 00:14:17,800
There's the textbook version, and then there's

337
00:14:17,800 --> 00:14:18,920
what they really do.

338
00:14:18,920 --> 00:14:20,560
So in the textbook, you might remember

339
00:14:20,560 --> 00:14:22,320
is something's a public good, and it's not

340
00:14:22,320 --> 00:14:24,120
a solutable and non-rival.

341
00:14:24,120 --> 00:14:26,400
But when you actually hear the things that economists

342
00:14:26,400 --> 00:14:31,480
describe as public goods, often they are totally rival,

343
00:14:31,480 --> 00:14:34,880
and they're often also least partly solutable.

344
00:14:34,880 --> 00:14:37,320
So then why are they called the public goods?

345
00:14:37,320 --> 00:14:38,960
That's kind of odd.

346
00:14:38,960 --> 00:14:43,880
But anyway, so I have a pet theory that explains,

347
00:14:43,880 --> 00:14:45,840
well, at least I think it explains generally

348
00:14:45,840 --> 00:14:48,920
when do economists stop talking about positive externalities

349
00:14:48,920 --> 00:14:51,720
and start talking about public goods.

350
00:14:51,720 --> 00:14:55,880
And my pet theory is that people switch over just when,

351
00:14:55,880 --> 00:14:57,680
like in the case of positive externalities,

352
00:14:57,680 --> 00:15:01,120
they switch over when the social benefits are huge compared

353
00:15:01,120 --> 00:15:03,680
to actual market event.

354
00:15:03,680 --> 00:15:06,200
So basically, in practice, economists

355
00:15:06,200 --> 00:15:09,160
treat public goods as an extreme kind of positive externalities.

356
00:15:09,160 --> 00:15:11,960
It would be something like this, where this is supply,

357
00:15:11,960 --> 00:15:14,440
and then demand is basically down there,

358
00:15:14,440 --> 00:15:17,840
and then social benefits are up here.

359
00:15:17,840 --> 00:15:23,880
So this is when economists actually generally switch over

360
00:15:23,880 --> 00:15:24,560
to public goods.

361
00:15:24,560 --> 00:15:25,920
So basically, demand is really low.

362
00:15:25,920 --> 00:15:27,500
It doesn't need to literally be there.

363
00:15:27,500 --> 00:15:30,520
But if there's very little market demand,

364
00:15:30,520 --> 00:15:32,400
so that the intersection of supply and demand

365
00:15:32,400 --> 00:15:35,200
would be somewhere around zero, but there's still

366
00:15:35,200 --> 00:15:37,480
some potential social benefits, then they switch over

367
00:15:37,480 --> 00:15:40,000
and call it a public good.

368
00:15:40,000 --> 00:15:44,840
So like the Swiss military during World War II.

369
00:15:44,840 --> 00:15:47,000
So is that rival?

370
00:15:47,000 --> 00:15:47,720
Sure.

371
00:15:47,720 --> 00:15:51,760
So there's a legal facility.

372
00:15:51,760 --> 00:15:54,220
If you go and put more defenses in one area of Switzerland,

373
00:15:54,220 --> 00:15:56,720
there's other places that are going to be less dependent.

374
00:15:56,720 --> 00:15:59,000
And then even for excludability, there's

375
00:15:59,000 --> 00:16:00,920
something, some kinds of defense that you

376
00:16:00,920 --> 00:16:05,200
can do where we'll actually defend some people rather

377
00:16:05,200 --> 00:16:06,400
than others.

378
00:16:06,400 --> 00:16:10,800
But the main thing is just that there's almost no demand.

379
00:16:10,800 --> 00:16:12,080
There's almost no market demand.

380
00:16:12,080 --> 00:16:13,600
If you went past the hat, you'd be

381
00:16:13,600 --> 00:16:16,920
like a Swiss hat with a feather in it, for contributions,

382
00:16:16,920 --> 00:16:18,800
it would probably be very little compared

383
00:16:18,800 --> 00:16:20,240
to the social benefits of not being

384
00:16:20,240 --> 00:16:22,360
dominated by the Nazis.

385
00:16:22,360 --> 00:16:24,600
And then similarly, if you'll buy this

386
00:16:24,600 --> 00:16:26,920
as what public goods are, then I'd

387
00:16:26,920 --> 00:16:31,320
say public goods are really just in practice.

388
00:16:31,320 --> 00:16:34,240
So this is a description of how economists really use the word

389
00:16:34,240 --> 00:16:37,360
or really use the phrase rather than what the textbook says.

390
00:16:37,360 --> 00:16:39,400
And maybe the textbook just doesn't accurately

391
00:16:39,400 --> 00:16:42,440
explain what economists switch from between the terms.

392
00:16:42,440 --> 00:16:45,040
But anyway, so that's a case where you've got supply.

393
00:16:45,040 --> 00:16:46,540
And the public bad is the case where

394
00:16:46,540 --> 00:16:49,000
you've got substantial demand, but where

395
00:16:49,000 --> 00:16:52,840
the social benefits are basically around zero.

396
00:16:52,840 --> 00:16:55,000
So you can think of public bads as the bad.

397
00:16:55,000 --> 00:16:57,240
Public bads are things where if you could just

398
00:16:57,240 --> 00:16:59,640
wave a wand to make the thing impossible,

399
00:16:59,640 --> 00:17:01,960
it would actually be socially beneficial.

400
00:17:01,960 --> 00:17:04,000
Like for example, you could say murder for hire

401
00:17:04,000 --> 00:17:06,680
is a public bad.

402
00:17:06,680 --> 00:17:09,760
Do you want to leave some murder for hire around?

403
00:17:09,760 --> 00:17:11,680
Which is very different from, say, pollution.

404
00:17:11,680 --> 00:17:13,840
For pollution, if you say, let's go and wave a wand

405
00:17:13,840 --> 00:17:15,920
and make it impossible for anyone to pollute.

406
00:17:15,920 --> 00:17:17,240
Notice that's different from waving a wand

407
00:17:17,240 --> 00:17:18,620
and getting rid of the pollution.

408
00:17:18,620 --> 00:17:19,440
That's always good.

409
00:17:19,440 --> 00:17:21,240
If you wave a wand, which prevents anyone

410
00:17:21,240 --> 00:17:23,160
from using a polluting technology.

411
00:17:23,160 --> 00:17:25,120
Suppose we were to do this today.

412
00:17:25,120 --> 00:17:27,120
We wave a wand and anyone who tries

413
00:17:27,120 --> 00:17:30,880
to use a polluting technology dies on the spot.

414
00:17:30,880 --> 00:17:32,920
Now, what would happen to society

415
00:17:32,920 --> 00:17:36,560
within a matter of months if we did this without any warning?

416
00:17:36,560 --> 00:17:38,440
First person that goes to the bathroom dies.

417
00:17:38,440 --> 00:17:38,940
Yes.

418
00:17:38,940 --> 00:17:41,640
Yeah, well, that too.

419
00:17:41,640 --> 00:17:43,000
Suppose those like fossil fuels.

420
00:17:43,000 --> 00:17:47,080
Suppose we ban any use of fossil fuels.

421
00:17:47,080 --> 00:17:52,200
Well, within a few months, most of us would just be dead.

422
00:17:52,200 --> 00:17:53,640
If you had a lot of warning, maybe

423
00:17:53,640 --> 00:17:55,440
we'd develop some alternative technologies.

424
00:17:55,440 --> 00:17:56,840
But without any warning, this would

425
00:17:56,840 --> 00:17:58,560
destroy the entire transportation system,

426
00:17:58,560 --> 00:17:59,960
the entire crop harvesting system,

427
00:17:59,960 --> 00:18:02,220
the entire, like really the whole agricultural system,

428
00:18:02,220 --> 00:18:03,640
all depends upon fossil fuels.

429
00:18:03,640 --> 00:18:07,000
So if you would go and ban the pollutants without warning,

430
00:18:07,000 --> 00:18:09,320
it would just kill most of the people on Earth.

431
00:18:09,320 --> 00:18:11,440
On the other hand, if you just ban murder for hire,

432
00:18:11,440 --> 00:18:13,600
what's the downside of that?

433
00:18:13,600 --> 00:18:14,840
You just stop it, prevent it.

434
00:18:14,840 --> 00:18:17,240
No one can do it anymore.

435
00:18:17,240 --> 00:18:18,920
Or you could stop murder.

436
00:18:18,920 --> 00:18:20,640
If we stop murder, then we won't be

437
00:18:20,640 --> 00:18:23,160
able to threaten to execute anyone for murder anymore.

438
00:18:23,160 --> 00:18:25,880
Well, at least we won't need to, because there

439
00:18:25,880 --> 00:18:28,280
will be no more murder.

440
00:18:28,280 --> 00:18:31,280
So you think of the regular negative externalities

441
00:18:31,280 --> 00:18:34,480
are things where there's a good with a negative side effect,

442
00:18:34,480 --> 00:18:36,640
but still it is worthwhile having the good exist

443
00:18:36,640 --> 00:18:38,080
to some degree.

444
00:18:38,080 --> 00:18:40,880
And the public bads are ones where they're so worthless

445
00:18:40,880 --> 00:18:44,360
that if you could simply prevent their existence,

446
00:18:44,360 --> 00:18:46,320
not preventing the existence of the bad things,

447
00:18:46,320 --> 00:18:48,780
that's trivial, but prevent the existence of the thing that

448
00:18:48,780 --> 00:18:51,400
is a bad thing, then with public bads,

449
00:18:51,400 --> 00:18:56,200
actually, it would be totally worthwhile if you could do it.

450
00:18:56,200 --> 00:18:58,280
So questions about this?

451
00:18:58,280 --> 00:19:00,560
So again, I am well aware that this is not,

452
00:19:00,560 --> 00:19:01,840
but it's in textbooks.

453
00:19:01,840 --> 00:19:04,280
I'm just claiming that if you listen carefully

454
00:19:04,280 --> 00:19:06,480
when economists stop just saying positive or negative

455
00:19:06,480 --> 00:19:09,760
externalities and switch to public goods and public bads,

456
00:19:09,760 --> 00:19:12,080
usually they switch when it's an extreme case,

457
00:19:12,080 --> 00:19:13,520
where for public goods, they think

458
00:19:13,520 --> 00:19:15,440
there'll be almost no market demand,

459
00:19:15,440 --> 00:19:17,040
and for public bads, where they think

460
00:19:17,040 --> 00:19:20,040
there's no social value.

461
00:19:20,040 --> 00:19:22,000
So of course, maybe some of you like textbooks,

462
00:19:22,000 --> 00:19:24,120
and then you would exchange what the textbooks say.

463
00:19:24,120 --> 00:19:26,080
And then this will all be clear.

464
00:19:34,040 --> 00:19:36,840
Now, just some things to notice about this.

465
00:19:36,840 --> 00:19:39,800
So one is, suppose you've got a monopoly.

466
00:19:42,360 --> 00:19:43,800
What would happen if it were like,

467
00:19:43,800 --> 00:19:45,920
would it be better or worse if the monopoly also

468
00:19:45,920 --> 00:19:47,800
had a negative externality?

469
00:19:47,800 --> 00:19:49,000
Yes, better.

470
00:19:49,000 --> 00:19:50,080
Why?

471
00:19:50,080 --> 00:19:52,040
Because they'd be producing less of the goods.

472
00:19:52,040 --> 00:19:53,200
Yes, yes.

473
00:19:53,200 --> 00:19:56,720
So intuitively, people think of a polluting monopolist

474
00:19:56,720 --> 00:20:16,720
as being worse than something that's only a monopoly.


